# Trading365 — agent rules

## Scope discipline (hard rule)

Build exactly what was asked. Nothing else. Do not expand the task.

**Never do these unless explicitly requested in the message:**

- **Before/after comparisons.** No old-vs-new output diffs, no "here's how the
  previous version rendered", no side-by-side. If the user wants a comparison they
  will ask for one. Reconstructing legacy code from memory to show a diff is the
  single most expensive thing to do in this repo — it burns context and produces
  something nobody asked to read.
- **Comparison harnesses.** Don't build tooling whose purpose is to demonstrate a
  change. Demonstrate the change by showing the new output.
- **Extra assertions and guards.** If the ask is "render the new shapes", render the
  new shapes. Don't bolt on HTML-safety sweeps, promise-language regexes, tamper
  checks, or exhaustive input matrices on your own initiative.
- **Wider test coverage than requested.** One example per shape, not every
  permutation of every shape.
- **Generalising a fix.** If one file needs a change, change that file.

When tempted to add something "while I'm here": stop, don't. Mention it in one line
at the end as an option instead.

## Working directory (hard rule)

This repo lives at `C:\dev\trading365`. It must never live inside OneDrive
(`C:\Users\Lee\OneDrive\...`).

- OneDrive dehydrates files into cloud placeholders. Anything that walks the tree
  file-by-file must re-download each file first, and Windows refuses to
  rename or delete while OneDrive holds handles. Moving this repo out of OneDrive
  ran at ~158 files/minute — a job that should have been instant — and the
  original `Move-Item` failed outright with "used by another process".
- **Never run `next build`, `next dev`, `pnpm install`, `tsc`, or anything else
  that writes a large tree while the working directory is under OneDrive.** If the
  cwd resolves to a OneDrive path, stop and say so rather than building.
- `.next` is the worst offender: tens of thousands of small files, every one
  synced. A build there also intermittently fails with `EPERM: unlink` when
  OneDrive locks a file mid-build.
- If a build ever fails with `EPERM` or a rename/delete is refused, check the path
  first — it is almost certainly this, not the code.

## Verification

Verify with the smallest thing that proves the work: run the script, run `tsc` on
the touched files, confirm the build. Don't invent a verification framework.

## Writing files

The `editor` tool caps a single write at ~6000 characters. Estimate length BEFORE
writing and split into chunks up front. Do not discover the limit by failing — every
failed write is a full wasted round-trip.

## PowerShell and file encoding (this bit me — read before editing files via shell)

`Get-Content` and `Out-File` in Windows PowerShell 5.1 default to the **ANSI
codepage, not UTF-8**. Reading a UTF-8 source file with `Get-Content` and writing
it back with `Out-File -Encoding utf8` silently converts every non-ASCII
character to mojibake. An em-dash became `â€"` in `app/scanner/page.tsx` and
shipped to production before anyone noticed.

- **NEVER** use `Get-Content` / `Out-File` / `Set-Content` to rewrite a source
  file that may contain non-ASCII. Use:
  ```powershell
  $c = [System.IO.File]::ReadAllText($p, [System.Text.Encoding]::UTF8)
  [System.IO.File]::WriteAllText($p, $c, (New-Object System.Text.UTF8Encoding($false)))
  ```
  `ReadAllText($p)` without an explicit encoding is also risky depending on the
  host — always pass UTF8.
- `Out-File -Encoding utf8` additionally writes a BOM, which source files should
  not have.
- Prefer the editor tool for anything containing `—`, `→`, `’`, `€` or accents.
- **Mojibake check before committing** — any hit is a bug:
  `Select-String -Path <files> -Pattern '\u00E2'`
- Recognise the symptom on sight in rendered HTML: `â€"`, `â€™`, `â†'`, `Â·`.

## Writing style

Write in plain English. The reader is the repo owner, not a compiler.

- Lead with the answer. Reasoning after, and only if it earns its place.
- Short sentences. One idea each.
- No jargon, no internal shorthand, no invented labels for things.
- Do not paste raw command output, JSON blobs or big tables unless asked for them.
  Say what the result was in a sentence.
- Skip the hedging and the long parentheticals.
- Don't narrate what you are about to do or what you just did at length. Do it,
  then say what happened.
- Say "no" or "I couldn't do that" plainly and immediately, not buried at the end.

## Known repo noise (not your problem, don't try to fix)

- `npx tsc --noEmit` reports ~140 pre-existing errors. They cluster in `app/admin/*`,
  `app/api/scanner/_core.ts` (3 errors, TS2339/TS7053 on `.length` / index `[0]`),
  `app/api/llms/route.ts`, `lib/db.ts`, `lib/data/articles-db.ts`,
  `components/admin/*`, and the separate `trading365-ops/` sub-project, which
  tsconfig sweeps in via `"include": ["**/*.ts"]`. Pre-existing, unrelated to most
  work. Do NOT read the count as a regression signal — check whether any error names
  a file you touched instead. (An earlier version of this note said "~20"; that was
  wrong, it came from reading only the first 20 lines of tsc output.)
- `next.config.mjs` sets `ignoreBuildErrors`, so `npx next build` stays green
  despite those errors.
- `node` exits 1 when its `MODULE_TYPELESS_PACKAGE_JSON` warning is piped through
  PowerShell — that is stderr, not a real failure. Redirect stderr to check the real
  exit code.
- PowerShell console output mangles UTF-8 emoji/dashes into mojibake. The files are
  fine; don't "fix" encoding in response to console output.
